problems.problem:
Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $ABC$. The points $A_{1} 
eq A$, $B_{1} 
eq B$ and $C_{1} 
eq C$ lie, respectively, on the circumcircles of triangles $BCH$, $CAH$ and $ABH$ and satisfy $A_{1}H=B_{1}H=C_{1}H$. Denote by $H_{1}$, $H_{2}$ and $H_{3}$ the orthocenters of triangles $A_{1}BC$, $B_{1}CA$ and $C_{1}AB$, respectively. Prove that triangles $A_{1}B_{1}C_{1}$ and $H_{1}H_{2}H_{3}$ have the same orthocenter.

Find all $k>0$ for which there exists a function $f : [0,1]\times[0,1] \to [0,1]$ satisfying the following conditions for all $x,y,z \in [0,1]$:

$f(f(x,y),z)=f(x,f(y,z))$;

$f(x,y) = f(y,x)$;

$f(x,1)=x$;

$f(zx,zy) = z^{k}f(x,y)$.

Two points $M$ and $N$ are chosen inside a non-equilateral triangle $ABC$ such that $\angle BAM=\angle CAN$, $\angle ABM=\angle CBN$ and $$AM\cdot AN\cdot BC=BM\cdot BN\cdot CA=CM\cdot CN\cdot AB=k$$ for some real $k$. Prove that:

a) $3k=AB\cdot BC\cdot CA$;

b) the midpoint of $MN$ is the centroid of triangle $ABC$.
